text:
sasha: quick note — we've been seeing nondeterministic evict/refetch cycles in the canary after a leader failover. i think the root cause is our reliance on local recency heuristics during handoff. proposal: add a tiny "evict_ticket" (128-bit) to prefix residency records and use ticket ordering + a soft-pin window to decide eviction ordering across nodes. keeps global ordering deterministic during transfers.

mike: hmm — can you sketch how a ticket is assigned? is it monotonic at the node or global? :thinking:

sasha: simplest: issuing node attaches a ticket from a node-local monotonic counter + node id. when a prefix is transferred, the ticket is copied along. eviction removes lowest (oldest) ticket first, but we still expose a configurable LRU fallback for memory pressure.

anna: curious about read-after-write — if we copy tickets, what prevents an old ticket from keeping a stale prefix alive forever on the new owner? we need a way to advance or reissue tickets on hot access.

sasha: good point. idea: access bumps an "access_epoch" (8-byte) in the record; eviction considers (ticket, access_epoch) tuple ordering: primarily by access_epoch recency, secondarily by ticket age for tie-break. ticket just gives deterministic stability during transfers; epoch gives accessibility ordering.

jordan: does this interact with our prefilling/reactive-rehydrate flows? if we copy a ticket during handoff, we must ensure background rehydrate doesn't create lower-priority duplicates. Also how do we GC stale tickets on crash recovery?

sasha: for rehydrate, background fills should request a new ticket from the new owner if they intend to keep the slot; otherwise they mark as transient. for GC: when a node restarts it publishes a tombstone with its last-issued counter range. other nodes use that to bump any tickets from that node older than tombstone. essentially: tombstone advances a per-node minimum counter — old tickets below that are treated as expired for eviction ordering.

mike: can you show a tiny pseudo API for this? helps me think about implementation points.

anna: +1 for an API sketch.

sasha: sure — super rough:

```
struct Ticket { u64 node_id; u64 counter; }
struct Residency { Key prefix; Ticket ticket; u64 access_epoch; bool soft_pin; }

// owner node assigns:
Ticket issue_ticket(Node n) { return {n.id, ++n.counter}; }

// eviction comparator:
// newer = higher access_epoch
// tie-break: higher counter (newer ticket) wins
bool evict_less(Residency a, Residency b) {
  if (a.access_epoch != b.access_epoch) return a.access_epoch < b.access_epoch;
  return (a.ticket.node_id, a.ticket.counter) < (b.ticket.node_id, b.ticket.counter);
}
```

jordan: looks straightforward. is the access_epoch global? how is it advanced cheaply on hot paths without causing contention?

sasha: access_epoch is per-shard monotonic wallclock-ish counter. we can do relaxed increments per core and occasionally write a compacted epoch. goal is not perfect global ordering but coarse recency buckets to prefer recently touched prefixes.

mike: this reduces randomness — nice. what's the rollout plan? risky to change eviction comparator globally.

sasha: rollout plan: 1) nonbinding mode: advertise tickets + epochs but keep old eviction policy. 2) telemetry: measure handoff-induced refetchs. 3) gradual opt-in per deployment via runtime flag per shard. 4) if stable, flip default.

anna: metrics to watch: per-prefix refetch rate after handoff, handoff latency, tail latency spikes post-failover. also need a small story for backwards compatibility between nodes running old vs new logic.

sasha: compatibility: if old node transfers to new node, ticket still exists in record — both sides can interpret ticket. if new node transfers back to old node, old node just treats ticket as opaque counter; its eviction will still consider access_epoch. we only rely on ticket ordering when both sides opt-in, but ticket presence doesn't break the old code.

infra-bot: deploy-bot: note — there's a CI job (kv-cache/sim/evict-tickets) flaking intermittently, run 3/3 passed on latest commit but a single seed failed yesterday. see https://ci.internal/12345

mike: thanks infra-bot. anna/jordan — do you foresee a risk of ticket-counter wraparound or large node counts? node_id is stable u64; counter is 64-bit so wrap is unlikely in practice. we can add epoch windows to mitigate.

jordan: one more correctness thought: concurrent handoff + eviction race. if a prefix is scheduled for eviction at source while it's being handed off, we must ensure either the new owner sees a pin or the eviction is aborted. maybe a tiny handoff handshake: source marks residency as 'transferring' and only after transfer ack does it clear local residency. handshake also copies ticket.

sasha: yep — handshake sketch: source sets TRANSFERRING flag + last_ticket; destination pulls record, issues ACK and adopts ownership. eviction checks TRANSFERRING and defers until transfer completes or a transfer timeout.

anna: sounds doable. let's file a small RFC with the sketches + test plan: unit tests for comparator, crash recovery tombstones, and integration chaos test: leader failover while background rehydrate + eviction under pressure.

sasha: will do. thanks for quick feedback. i'll drop a follow-up RFC link here once written. :thumbsup:
